Q: Passing structure containing unions as a function argument A: <blockquote>
<p>my struct members don't get recognised nor can i get them initialised.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Evidently you mean (in part) that you cannot refer to the member union's members with an expression of the form <code>outer_struct_ptr-&gt;inner_struct_name</code>, omitting the name (<code>ip6_ctlun</code>) of the member of the outer struct whose member <code>inner_struct_name</code> is. It's unclear why you think you should be able to do that (but see below).  Your <code>struct Ip6_hdr</code> type has these members:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>ip6_ctlun</code> (untagged union type)</li>
<li><code>ip6_src</code> (<code>struct In6_addr</code>)</li>
<li><code>ip6_dst</code> (<code>struct In6_addr</code>)</li>
<li><code>set6</code> (a function pointer)</li>
</ul>
<p>Those are the names that are valid on the right-hand side of your <code>k-&gt;</code> operations, so to access the members of the <code>ip6_ctlun</code> members, you would use lvalues such as <code>k-&gt;ip6_ctlun.ip6_un1</code>.</p>
<p>Similar applies to the <code>struct</code>-type member of the member union. You need to specify its name to traverse it to get to its members. Overall, then, instead of <code>k-&gt;ip6_un1_flow</code>, you're looking for</p>
<pre><code>   k           -&gt;  ip6_ctlun         . ip6_un1             . ip6_un1_flow
// an Ip6_hdr*
//                 an untagged union
//                                     a struct ip6_hdrctl 
//                                                           a uint32_t
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>With that said, C does have a way for <code>struct</code> and <code>union</code> types to provide <em>anonymous</em> nested <code>struct</code>s and <code>union</code>s, whose members you can access directly from the top level.  Possibly that's what confused you. Anonymous <code>struct</code>s and <code>union</code>s not only must not have member names (which is why they are &quot;anonymous&quot;), but their type specifiers must be untagged <code>struct</code> or <code>union</code> specifiers.  Supposing that you do not rely on the <code>struct ip6_hdrctl</code> type declared inside <code>struct Ip6_hdr</code> for anything else, you could convert your type declaration to match your attempted usage like so:</p>
<pre><code>struct Ip6_hdr {
    union /* never was a tag here */ {
        struct /* no tag here */ {
            uint32_t ip6_un1_flow;   /* 4 bits version, 8 bits TC, 20 bits flow-ID */
            uint16_t ip6_un1_plen;   /* payload length */
            uint8_t  ip6_un1_nxt;    /* next header */
            uint8_t  ip6_un1_hlim;   /* hop limit */
        } /* no member name here */;
        uint8_t ip6_un2_vfc;         /* 4 bits version, top 4 bits tclass */
    } /* no member name here */;
    struct In6_addr ip6_src;         /* source address */
    struct In6_addr ip6_dst;         /* destination address */
    void (*set6)(struct Ip6_hdr*, union ip6_ctlun*, struct ip6_hdrctl*,  uint32_t , uint16_t, uint8_t, uint8_t, uint8_t, uint16_t,uint32_t, uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t);
};
</code></pre>
<p>With that, you might indeed write <code>k-&gt;ip6_un1_flow</code>, where <code>k</code> has type <code>struct Ip6_hdr *</code>.</p>
<p>Note that the <code>typedef</code> and <code>__packed</code> in the original declaration combined to have the effect of declaring <code>__packed</code> as an alias for type <code>struct Ip6_hdr</code>.  Or at least they would do if <code>__packed</code> were not a reserved identifier.  I'm inclined to guess that <code>__packed</code> was intended to be taken instead as an extension keyword specifying structure packing, but if the compiler interpreted it that way then the <code>typedef</code> declaration would be faulty on account of not declaring any identifiers.</p>
<p>My personal practice and recommendation is to reserve <code>typedef</code>s for cases that provide a significant improvement to code readability.  I rarely <code>typedef</code> structure and union types, but function types are a different matter.  For example, I would be inclined to go with this alternative version of the above:</p>
<pre><code>struct Ip6_hdr;  // forward declaration
typedef void set6_func(struct Ip6_hdr*, union ip6_ctlun*, struct ip6_hdrctl*, uint32_t,
        uint16_t, uint8_t, uint8_t, uint8_t, uint16_t,uint32_t, uint8_t, uint16_t, uint32_t);

struct Ip6_hdr {
    union /* never was a tag here */ {
        struct /* no tag here */ {
            uint32_t ip6_un1_flow;   /* 4 bits version, 8 bits TC, 20 bits flow-ID */
            uint16_t ip6_un1_plen;   /* payload length */
            uint8_t  ip6_un1_nxt;    /* next header */
            uint8_t  ip6_un1_hlim;   /* hop limit */
        } /* no member name here */;
        uint8_t ip6_un2_vfc;         /* 4 bits version, top 4 bits tclass */
    } /* no member name here */;
    struct In6_addr ip6_src;         /* source address */
    struct In6_addr ip6_dst;         /* destination address */
    set6_func *set6;
};
</code></pre>
<p>That would be improved even more by renaming the <code>set6_func</code> type to something more meaningful and giving it meaningful parameter names.</p>

Q: CSS3 ignore Child element when animating A: <p>I worked around this by doing the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class=&quot;icon-wrap icon-effect&quot;&gt;
  &lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;icoback&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;arrow-down&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then applying some css to .icoback. See the fiddle for details: <a href="http://jsfiddle.net/xtW22/2/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://jsfiddle.net/xtW22/2/</a></p>
